.TH CEVENTLOOP_DESTROY 3 "2026-08-02" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ceventloop_destroy \- destroy a ceventloop and invalidate the handle
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ceventloop_destroy(loop)
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Waits until every resolved use of the handle that is in flight has
finished, shuts every reactor thread down (unless that already
happened) and frees every registration that is left. At the end it sets
loop to CEVENTLOOP_NULL.

The shutdown is the same graceful drain as ceventloop_shutdown(3).
The callbacks that the loop has already collected still run, and loop
stays a live handle for them until the drain ends, so a callback can
call ceventloop_remove(3), ceventloop_pause(3),
ceventloop_resume(3), ceventloop_modify(3) and
ceventloop_add(3) on loop during the destroy, with the same results
as during ceventloop_shutdown(3). That makes it safe to close a
connection from its own callback (remove each of its registrations,
close the fd, free arg) while another thread destroys the loop: Once
the removals return, no callback of those registrations runs again.
After the drain, loop stops resolving and every later call on it
fails as it would for a destroyed handle.

loop must be a handle that is live at the time of the call, or
CEVENTLOOP_NULL, which does nothing. A call on a stale handle
(one that an earlier call destroyed, or that another thread is
destroying at the same time) is a FATAL ERROR (abort()/SIGABRT) and
never a silent double free. The handle carries a generation tag that is
checked before the loop is touched, so such a value is always caught
and never dereferenced.

Calling it on loop from a callback running on one of loop's reactor
threads or dispatch threads is a FATAL ERROR (abort()/SIGABRT) too,
rather than a deadlock or a silent no-op. Hand the destroy to another
thread, or do it after the callback returns.

A queue-backed registration (one built with
ccircq_selectable(), cdynmq_selectable() or
cchan_selectable()) is unlinked from its queue's waiter list
as it is freed, so a queue that outlives this ceventloop is never
left with a dangling waiter pointer. The reverse order is the caller's
job: Do not destroy a queue while a registration for it is live.
ccircq_destroy(3), cdynmq_destroy(3) and
cchan_destroy(3) detect that case and assert against it.

Each remaining registration has a handlers.on_removed field (see
ceventloop_add(3)). When it is set, it fires exactly once as part
of the free, the same guarantee that an ordinary
ceventloop_remove() call gives a live registration.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
loop  An lvalue of type ceventloop.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The destroy never closes a registered fd and never destroys a
registered queue or cchan. Each one stays with its owner, just as
ceventloop_remove(3) leaves it.

loop is evaluated exactly once and must be a modifiable lvalue, such as
a variable or an array element. An expression with a side effect, such
as handles[i++], therefore destroys and clears a single element.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ceventloop_create (3),
.BR ceventloop_shutdown (3),
.BR ceventloop_remove (3)
